 # Sparse Voxel Tree (SVO Tree)
 
-Also called: ESVO, Hierarchical Voxel Storage, Sparse voxel octrees, Voxel Tree
+Also called: ESVO, Hierarchical Voxel Storage, Sparse Voxel Octree (OpenVDB), Voxel Tree
 
 ## Overview
-A Sparse Voxel Tree (SVO) is a tree data structure for sparse voxel grids where all 3 dimensions are divided and each node has 8 leaves [S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of storing and querying large, sparse 3D volumes by allocating memory only for non-empty regions, providing compression proportional to sparsity [S2]. It sets itself apart from [Sparse Voxel DAGs](2791-sparse-voxel-dags.md) by allowing multiple paths to the same voxel (no sharing), and from [Compact pointer trees](844-compact-pointer-tree.md) by using a fixed branching factor of 8 rather than variable $N$ [G].
+A Sparse Voxel Tree (SVO) is a hierarchical data structure for representing sparse volumetric data, where the 3D domain is recursively subdivided into octants (nodes with 8 children) [S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the storage and traversal inefficiency of dense 3D grids by storing samples of occupancy or density fields only in leaf nodes where data exists, allowing ray marching to skip empty space efficiently [S3, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Sparse Voxel DAGs](2791-sparse-voxel-dags.md) which merge identical subtrees, the SVO retains a strict tree topology, though it often uses block-based memory layouts to facilitate dynamic updates [S2].
 
