 ### [Least Squares Surface Simplification](11029-least-squares-surface-simplification.md)
 Least Squares Surface Simplification is a method for reducing the resolution of a Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) by finding the best-fit scalar field values for a coarser voxel level. It solves the problem of data reduction by minimizing the energy difference between the prolonged coarse field and the original fine field, formulated as a least squares problem. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like Morton-coded SVOs by explicitly optimizing field values to minimize approximation error rather than relying solely on structural sparsity or hashing.
 
+### SVO Culling and Sorting
+Post-processing steps for the SVO: culling unfilled branches, breadth-sorting for cache locality, and depth-sorting for traversal order [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Cull unfilled:** Recursively traverse from root. If leaf node has color 0, return false (cull). If internal node, recursively cull children. If a child is culled, remove its bit from parent.occupancy. If parent becomes a leaf (only 1 child or 0 children depending on logic, code checks is_leaf after loop), return false. Otherwise update parent in nodes array and return true [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Breadth-sort:** Create new nodes vector. Queue root. While queue not empty, pop node_ref. Clone node. For each child, push child_ref to queue front, update child reference in node to cursor, increment cursor. If node has color, encode node_ref into color (shifted and masked). Push node to new nodes vector [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Depth-sort:** Create new nodes vector. Recursively traverse from root. For each node, create new reference (current nodes.len()), clone node, push to new nodes. Recursively update child references. Update node in new nodes array with updated children. Return new reference [S5, unofficial].
+
+### VOLA (Octree with branching factor 64)
+An efficient file format for storing quantized point cloud data with no attributes other than geometry, using an octree where two layers are combined into one [S6].
+*   Store voxels as an octree rather than an array [S6].
+*   Combine two layers of the octree into one, creating a tree with a branching factor of 64 [S6].
+
+### Sparse Voxel Octree Traversal
+A sparse voxel octree traversal method as described by Laine & Karras (2010) [S7, unofficial]. Performed well compared to other techniques in complex scenes, speculated due to improved cache coherency from more compact data storage [S7, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(\text{MAX\_DEPTH})$ for allocation, retrieval, and deallocation [S1, unofficial]. Since `MAX_DEPTH` is constant (3 to 10), operations are effectively $O(1)$ with a small constant factor [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of allocated voxels [S1, unofficial]. Each block holds exactly 8 elements (nodes or voxels) [S1, unofficial]. Overhead includes block metadata and free lists [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Requires about 5 bytes per voxel with color, normal, and contour, or 6.67 bytes per finest-level sample once the mip hierarchy is counted [S2]. If only surfaces are encoded, memory usage drops to $O(N^2)$ [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient for storage and rendering of sparse data [S2]. Dynamic updates are facilitated by block-based storage with relative pointers [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Traversal is worse than hierarchical-grid methods by as much as 60% [S2]. Some experts consider SVOs "good only for storage and rendering, and not even great at those" [S2]. Bulk allocation estimation is difficult; pre-allocation is avoided to prevent performance degradation from overestimation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(MAX\_DEPTH)$ for allocation, retrieval, and deallocation. Since `MAX_DEPTH` is constant (3 to 10), operations are effectively $O(1)$ with a small constant factor [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of allocated voxels. Each block holds exactly 8 elements (nodes or voxels). Overhead includes block metadata and free lists [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** ESVO requires about 5 bytes per voxel with color, normal, and contour, or 6.67 bytes per finest-level sample once the mip hierarchy is counted [S2]. If only surfaces are encoded, memory usage drops to $O(N^2)$ [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Adapts to the location of scene geometry, storing only leaf nodes where data exists, unlike dense 3D grids which require $4096^3$ cells (~128 GB for rgb$\sigma$) [S3, unofficial]. Octree data in blocks with relative pointers facilitates dynamic updates, such as streaming [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Octree traversal is worse than hierarchical-grid methods by as much as 60% [S2]. SVOs are considered good only for storage and rendering, and "not even great" at those tasks in some contexts [S2]. MSVC compilation can cause significant branch/pipeline overhead in traversal loops [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) is a tree data structure for sparse voxel grids where all 3 dimensions are divided and each node has 8 leaves [S3, unofficial]. It is used in techniques such as Interactive Indirect Illumination Using Voxel Cone Tracing, where it stores pre-filtered hierarchical voxel representations of scene geometry on the GPU to estimate visibility and integrate indirect energy [S6]. The structure is also foundational in Neural Sparse Voxel Fields, which learn sparse voxel grids progressively [S3, unofficial], and in PlenOctrees for NeRF-SH conversion [S3, unofficial].
+The Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) is a tree-structure for representing sparse volumetric data, inspired by B+ trees used in databases [S3, unofficial]. It was popularized by OpenVDB [S3, unofficial]. The method allows ray marching to skip empty space efficiently by storing samples of occupancy or density fields in the tree structure instead of triangles [S3, unofficial]. Laine & Karras (2010) presented a sparse voxel octree-based technique for representing geometry and shading attributes, which is cited as a key reference for the ESVO format [S2][S7, unofficial].
 
